// SDL's own structs, named from the layer an application imports.
//
// **A transparent alias is a second spelling, not a second type.** `type Rect = c.Rect` declares
// nothing of its own: a value built here is the value `c` builds, crosses to C through the same
// pointer, and satisfies both names everywhere one of them is written. This file therefore costs
// nothing at run time and exists so that a program drawing a rectangle can name one.
//
// **Why an alias rather than a move.** `constants.sysl` moved SDL's three hundred numbers up here
// outright, because nothing in `c` ever read one. These are the opposite case: `c`'s own
// declarations take them — `SDL_RenderFillRect` is written against a `*Rect` — and a module may not
// depend on a module that depends on it. So the declaration stays below and is named from above,
// which is the shape the two-layer split needs and could not express until 0.0.67.
//
// **`Surface` is deliberately not here.** This module already declares an owning `Surface` in
// `texture.sysl` that frees what it points at when the last reference goes; SDL's raw one is a
// different thing wearing the same name, and giving it that name here would hide the owning one.
// `sdl3-ttf` is the only caller that needs the raw type and it still reaches `c` for it.

module sh.sysl.sdl3

import sh.sysl.sdl3.c

// `SDL_Rect` — integer screen coordinates. `rect` builds one without naming the type.
type Rect = c.Rect

// `SDL_FRect` — the floating-point coordinates the renderer works in, which is every drawing call.
// `frect` builds one without naming the type.
type FRect = c.FRect

// `SDL_FPoint` — a point in those same coordinates.
type FPoint = c.FPoint

// `SDL_Vertex` — position, colour in 0..1 and texture coordinates, eight contiguous floats, which is
// what `Renderer.geometry` hands to `SDL_RenderGeometry`.
type Vertex = c.Vertex

// `SDL_AudioSpec` — sample format, channel count and rate.
type AudioSpec = c.AudioSpec

// `SDL_DialogFileFilter` — a description and a pattern, for the file dialogs in `dialog.sysl`.
type DialogFileFilter = c.DialogFileFilter
